Map, filter, and flat-map.

Three intermediate operations do the bulk of the work in almost every pipeline. Filter decides which elements continue, map decides what each one becomes, and flat-map handles the awkward case where each element expands into many. Learn these three shapes, and most of stream programming is just assembling them.

Start with filter. It takes a predicate — a function from an element to a boolean, from module six. Each element that tests true continues; each false is discarded. The type never changes, and the count only ever goes down. So over the numbers one through six, filter where n modulo two equals zero keeps two, four, and six — still integers, just fewer of them. Think of filter as a gate: the same kind of things flow, but some don't make it through.

Now map. It takes a function — from an element to some new value. It applies that function to every element, producing a stream of the results. The count is unchanged, one output per input, but the type can change entirely. So a stream of names, mapped with string-dot-length, gives you three and five — that's a stream of integers now, not a stream of strings. Map the same names with to-uppercase, and you get the same type back, but with transformed values. So here's the contrast: filter changes how many; map changes what each one is. Almost every pipeline is a few of each — filter to the rows you want, then map them to the shape you need.

Here's the problem that flat-map solves. Sometimes the function you'd hand to map produces not one value, but a whole collection per element. Say you map a list of orders to their line-items. Each order gives you a list of items — so map leaves you with a stream of lists of items, when what you actually wanted was a flat stream of items. You're now stuck one level too deep. Every downstream operation would have to reach inside the inner list.

Flat-map fixes this. It takes a function that turns each element into a stream, and then concatenates all those little streams into one flat stream. It's map plus a flattening step, and the nesting just disappears. So instead of mapping each order to its list of items, you flat-map each order to its items as a stream — and you get a flat stream of items, one level deep. A classic example: splitting lines into words. Flat-map each line to a stream of its words, and you get one continuous stream of words across all the lines.

So the rule of thumb writes itself. If the transform gives you one value, reach for map. If it gives you many — a list, an array, an optional, a nested stream — reach for flat-map to keep the pipeline flat.

Let me put the three shapes side by side. Filter takes a predicate, element to boolean; it keeps the type and may reduce the count. Map takes a function, element to one value; it keeps the count and may change the type. And flat-map takes a function, element to a stream of values; it changes both, flattening one level of nesting away.
